steps.body:
The setup takes three imports from `java.io`, one reader wrapped around `System.in`, and a `throws` clause on `main`:

```stdin
3 9000000000
```

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));
        String line = br.readLine();               // null when input has ended
        String[] parts = line.strip().split("\\s+");
        int count = Integer.parseInt(parts[0]);
        long big = Long.parseLong(parts[1]);      // values beyond int
        System.out.println(count + " " + big);
    }
}
```

`readLine` can throw `IOException`, a checked exception, so `main` must declare `throws IOException`. The Exceptions lesson explains why. Use one reader per program, just like one `Scanner`: mixing `Scanner` and `BufferedReader` on `System.in` loses input, because each one buffers lines the other never sees.

Always `strip()` first. `Integer.parseInt(" 5")` throws `NumberFormatException` because a space is not a digit, so a count line with a stray space breaks without it. Splitting has the same trap: `"  5 7".split("\\s+")` gives `["", "5", "7"]`, and parsing that empty first token throws too. After `strip()`, `split("\\s+")` treats each run of spaces as one separator.

`readLine` returns `null` once input is used up, and calling `strip()` on `null` crashes. Only chain `br.readLine().strip()` when you know the line is there.

