When you do not know how many lines are coming, read until `readLine` returns `null`. This is the `BufferedReader` version of `while (sc.hasNextLine())`. The assignment sits inside the condition, so each pass reads one line and stops at the end. Here `br` is a `BufferedReader` on `System.in` and `counts` is a `TreeMap<String, Integer>`:

```stdin
The cat saw the dog

  the end  
```

```java harness
import java.util.*;
import java.io.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));
        Map<String, Integer> counts = new TreeMap<>();
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(counts);
    }
}
```

```java
String line;
while ((line = br.readLine()) != null) {
    if (line.isBlank()) {
        continue;                         // empty or only spaces
    }
    for (String word : line.strip().split("\\s+")) {
        String key = word.toLowerCase();  // "The" and "the" match
        counts.put(key, counts.getOrDefault(key, 0) + 1);
    }
}
```

Skip blank lines first. `"".split("\\s+")` returns one empty token, so a blank line would count `""` as a word.

Pick the collection by what you print. A `TreeMap` keeps keys sorted, so looping over `entrySet()` prints in alphabetical order. A `TreeSet` keeps unique values sorted, and `println(set)` shows `[a, b]`. Normalize keys with `toLowerCase()` before you store them, or `Ana` and `ana` become two entries. A `HashMap` would also count correctly, but its order is unspecified, so use it only when you print a total.

